# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Git line-ending / binary policy.
#
# With core.autocrlf=true (as on this Windows box) Git treats PDFs, images and
# archives as text and rewrites LF<->CRLF inside them.  That changes their
# bytes, invalidates the sha256 digests recorded in provenance/*.json, and can
# corrupt the files on the next checkout.  Mark every binary asset as binary so
# Git never touches its bytes, and pin the SVG sources (whose sha256 is
# recorded in provenance) to LF.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
*.pdf    binary
*.png    binary
*.jpg    binary
*.jpeg   binary
*.zip    binary
*.xlsx   binary
*.docx   binary
*.svg    text eol=lf

# Vendor font binaries (03_论文撰写/paper/ieeeaccess/) --- CRLF rewriting would
# corrupt them.  The official IEEE Access template text files ship LF; pin them
# to LF so the vendored copies stay byte-identical to the upstream archive.
*.pfb    binary
*.tfm    binary
*.cls    text eol=lf
*.bst    text eol=lf
*.sty    text eol=lf
*.fd     text eol=lf
*.map    text eol=lf

# Shell scripts must keep LF: a CRLF checkout makes `bash build.sh` fail with
# "\r: command not found" (build.sh / reproduce.sh / verify_artifact.sh).
*.sh     text eol=lf

# Manuscript sources are pinned to CRLF. The submitted LaTeX package
# (`latex_source_files_submission.zip`) is built from the working tree and its
# every entry is compared byte-for-byte with that tree
# (provenance/SUBMISSION_SOURCE_SHA256.json, check_submission_zip.py). Without
# an explicit eol the checkout ending is whatever core.autocrlf happens to be,
# so the same package would "match" on this box and fail on a Linux clone --
# a false alarm for anyone verifying the archive. Pinning the ending makes the
# comparison machine-independent. The stored blobs stay LF; only the checkout
# and the exported tree carry CRLF.
*.tex    text eol=crlf
*.bib    text eol=crlf
*.bbl    text eol=crlf
